The unit-chain meta-tester builds a tiny consistent world (a solo stack unit plus a two-unit group), confirms the cohesion checker is quiet, injects one deliberate corruption, and requires the checker to fail. Each paragraph below is one of those spoofs.

Mixed coordinates leave a live unit with only one of its two position fields set, so it is neither a proper map head nor a proper null-xy follower.

An out-of-range map stack head points the tile overlay at a pool index the unit vector has never allocated, so the map claims a unit that cannot exist.

Unique map head places the same live unit as the stack head of two different tiles, so one body appears to occupy two places at once.

A tile-stack cycle makes the on-tile next links loop among co-located units so a stack walk never reaches a clean end.

A dead stack pointer leaves the map still naming a unit after that slot has been returned to the pool, so the overlay references garbage.

Stack coordinates mismatch keeps a unit on a tile’s stack while its recorded position names a different tile, breaking the rule that stack members share the tile they hang from.

An out-of-range group link makes a head’s follower pointer jump past the allocated pool, so the group chain cannot be followed safely.

A group cycle closes the follower chain back onto an earlier member so walking the group never terminates.

Dual group membership hangs the same follower off two different heads, so one unit belongs to two move-as-one chains.

A dead group pointer keeps a head linked to a follower after that follower’s pool slot was recycled, so the chain names a unit that no longer lives.

Head in the tail restores real coordinates on a unit that is still listed as a non-head follower, violating the rule that only the group head may be positioned on the map.

A stack unit also used as another group’s follower attaches a map-visible unit into a second head’s chain, so the same unit is claimed both as an independent stack presence and as someone else’s tail.

A positioned unit missing from the map keeps real coordinates in the vector while no tile stack mentions it, so the unit claims a place the overlay does not show.

An orphan tail clears the only group link that reached a null-xy follower, leaving a shadow unit that is neither on a stack nor in any group.

A null-xy tail installed as a map stack head puts a follower-shaped unit onto the tile overlay as if it were a positioned head, mixing the two axes that must stay exclusive.
